<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahalia Jackson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mahala Jackson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahalia Jackson was an American gospel singer, born in poverty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> in 1911, the granddaughter of enslaved people, who was central to the development and spread of gospel blues in Black churches. In Chicago she sang with the Johnson Singers and drew on Thomas Dorsey and Bessie Smith; her 1947 recording Move On Up a Little Higher sold two million copies, a first for gospel music. She became the first gospel recording artist to tour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, turned down offers to record secular music, and is credited with influencing rhythm and blues, soul and rock and roll singing. A supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, she sang at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> in 1963. She died in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahalia-jackson.jpg" alt="Mahalia Jackson" width="330" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Van Vechten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahalia_Jackson_1962,_van_Vechten,_LC-USZ62-91314_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mahalia Jackson</b>'s 1947 recording was widely broadcast, and during the civil rights movement she sang it for marchers; in 1964 Fannie Lou Hamer led marchers in Mississippi singing it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>Later recordings include those of Tommy Dorsey (1941), Odetta (1957), Johnny Mathis (1958), the Roger Wagner Chorale and <b>Mahalia Jackson</b> (1964), Bobby Womack (2012) and Beverly Glenn-Copeland (2020).</blockquote><a href="/songs/deep-river">Deep River</a></li><li><blockquote>Paul Robeson recorded it in 1925, and <b>Mahalia Jackson</b>, Clara Ward, Laurie London and Elvis Presley (1960) were among many who followed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/joshua-fit-the-battle-of-jericho">Joshua Fit the Battle of Jericho</a></li><li><blockquote>It has since been recorded by <b>Mahalia Jackson</b>, Louis Armstrong, Lena Horne, Paul Robeson, Harry James, Sam Cooke and many others; in 1962 Georgia Lee, the first Indigenous Australian artist to record blues songs, included it on her album.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nobody-knows-the-trouble-ive-seen">Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Seen</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mahalia Jackson</b> recorded it in 1956, and Odetta sang it at Carnegie Hall on 8 April 1960; her version was heard in Pier Paolo Pasolini's film The Gospel According to St. Matthew (1964).</blockquote><a href="/songs/sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child">Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</a></li></ul></section>
